{"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"boundary","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A cleanup ownership write failure can leave a cancelled agent running and writing to the checkout without a recovery record."},{"id":"F2","consequence":"boundary","trigger":"unusual","reason":"A persisted Linux cleanup record can match and kill an unrelated process after a reboot if its PID and boot-relative start tick collide."},{"id":"F3","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Recovery can wait on a zombie child before killing its stopped parent, leaving the run blocked."},{"id":"F4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An unresolved cleanup in a shared directory can permanently block an unrelated run even after the record is removed."},{"id":"F5","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Several unresolved cleanups can delay daemon health checks past the deploy deadline and cause a rollback."}]}